<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Read this first</p>
    <h2 id="t-the-question-physics-does-not-touch">The Question Physics Does Not Touch</h2>
    <p class="lede">Every physical theory opens its account with the curtain already raised: a stage already standing, laws already in force and fields already humming. Why there is a stage at all is usually set aside as metaphysics. The r3.5 argument refused that surrender and sought an answer in geometry; the integrated edition asks whether existence is better described as continuing differentiation and renewed integration.</p>

    <p>Take zero and one. In the original reading, zero is the floor no shrinking positive quantity reaches and one is the threshold of <em>this</em> rather than <em>that</em>. Between them lies an uncountable continuum: more real numbers than the countably infinite march of 1, 2, 3, 4&hellip; <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The mathematical result is established. Calling its ends physically different infinities, or reading the asymmetry as the first slope of time, is TTI&rsquo;s speculative interpretation.</p>

    <p>Then comes the historical question: <strong>how are zero and one joined?</strong> r3.5 answered that a straight path toward the infinitely small never closes and that closure therefore introduces π. The current theorem-led paper states the boundary more exactly: the numerals 0 and 1 alone do not force π, but once circular closure, Euclidean arc measure or the beta&ndash;gamma relation is supplied, π appears through exact relations on the unit interval.</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      e^{i\pi}+1=0,
      \qquad
      B\!\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac12\right)
      =\int_0^1\frac{dx}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}}=\pi
    \]</p>

    <p>The restored argument is therefore retained without hiding its debt: exact mathematics supports its ingredients, while the promotion from closure to ontology remains chosen. Its expanded conjectural sequence is that integration differentiates, the Rankin Skeletal Splice routes continuation and return, and a seam makes return conceivable without an absolute edge. The pages below mark every crossing between theorem, observation and vision.</p>

    <p class="muted small">Grading throughout: <span class="sci">&#9670; established result or observation</span> &middot;
      <span class="hist">&#9670; scholarship and history</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; speculative or contested bridge</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; vision, stated as vision</span>.</p>
    <p><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/pi-from-zero-and-one/">Read the theorem-led π paper</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

